  "historical_output_text": " Imagine that a friend asked about the rain we have been expecting this month because the skies have felt very cloudy and the wind feels stronger.\n\n**Friend’s question on weather:** \n“Have there really been signs in the air and the clouds today, suggesting we’ll have a rain day?”\n\nYou reply, explaining as if they are 45.\n\nWhen clouds build in a way we’ve seen lately – thick and low in layers like those in your pantry – they mean we have what meteorlogists often say as 'high probability conditions’ that rain will form and drop on our town.\n\n**What causes it to be so windy too:**  \nWind is often an indicator we might see more than the regular rain clouds and precipitation, as wind itself brings moisture, like a big wet sponge. As that wet, windy front pushes through, clouds start building,",
